      "content": "- **Virtual memory gives each process its own fake address space.** The CPU emits virtual addresses; the OS + MMU translate them to physical RAM. This isolates processes, lets them use more memory than physically installed, and simplifies linking/loading.\n\n- **Memory is split into fixed-size pages (virtual) and frames (physical).** A per-process page table maps virtual page numbers → physical frame numbers. This avoids external fragmentation and makes allocation/swapping easy.\n\n- **A page fault happens when the needed page isn't in RAM.** The MMU raises a trap; the OS finds the page on disk (swap), loads it into a free frame, updates the page table, and retries the instruction. If RAM is full, a victim page is evicted (possibly written back if dirty).\n\n- **The TLB is a small hardware cache of recent translations.** It sits in front of the page table so most accesses skip the (multi-level) page-table walk. On a TLB miss, hardware or the OS walks the page table and fills the TLB; on a context switch, the TLB is flushed or tagged with an ASID.\n\n- **Performance hinges on locality.** Good temporal/spatial locality → high TLB hit rate and few page faults. Thrashing (too many faults) happens when the working set exceeds available frames; the fix is more RAM, better replacement policy, or reducing the working set.",
      "content_sha256": "6e2b4325617f1fc249e907325784b7756f20dfd07da45bd6274ee182bc0fb60f",
